      <section>
        <h2>The Problem with Most "Big and Tall" Chair Listings</h2>
        <!-- tca-aio-capsule -->
        <p>TCA's big-and-tall chair analysis separates the two requirements that retailer listings tend to merge: weight capacity and tall-user dimensions. For users 6 feet to 6 feet 3 inches it recommends the Steelcase Gesture at a 21-inch seat height and 18.75-inch adjustable depth; for 6 feet 3 inches to 6 feet 7 inches with higher capacity needs, the Steelcase Leap Plus at 19.75-inch adjustable depth and a 500 lb rating — with the caveat that its 22.5-inch seat height requires the optional 5-inch cylinder, specified at order time. The standard cylinder stops at 19.5 inches.</p>
        <p>I'm Jackson Christopher — 6'4", Mechanical Engineering senior at UC Berkeley. I spent months researching ergonomic chairs before buying the Steelcase Gesture, and one of the things that frustrated me most about that process was the "big and tall" category. Most chairs labeled big-and-tall in retail listings add weight capacity and a wider seat — but they don't touch the spec that matters most for tall users: seat height.</p>
        <p>A chair with a 400 lb rating and a 20" seat height maximum is not a chair for tall people. It is a wide, reinforced chair built for heavier users of average height. For a user who is both tall and heavier — or simply tall and wanting a chair with structural headroom — those are different requirements that have to be evaluated separately.</p>
        <p>This guide breaks down the three dimensions that define a genuinely tall-appropriate chair and identifies which products in the big-and-tall category actually address all three.</p>
        <p class="bg-secondary/30 border-l-2 border-primary/40 pl-4 py-1 text-sm">
          <strong>Note on testing:</strong> I personally use and have tested the Steelcase Gesture. All other chairs in this guide are research-based assessments drawing on manufacturer specifications, engineering analysis, and aggregated user reports. Where I note "based on published specs" or "community reports suggest," that is the honest sourcing.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section>
        <h2>The Three Specs That Define a Chair for Tall Users</h2>

        <h3>1. Seat Height Maximum</h3>
        <p>Seat height is the distance from the floor to the top of the seat pan. The ergonomic requirement is that your thighs be parallel to the floor (or slightly declining) with your feet flat on the floor. At 6'0", this typically requires a seat height of 18"–20". At 6'3", 19"–21". At 6'5"+, 21"–23".</p>
        <p>Most chairs — including most labeled "big and tall" — max out at 20"–21". That covers users through about 6'3" in most proportional cases. It leaves users at 6'4"+ with their knees higher than their hips, which transfers load to the lumbar spine and causes the low-back fatigue that tall users disproportionately report after long desk sessions.</p>

        <h3>2. Seat Depth (Adjustable Range)</h3>
        <p>Seat depth is the front-to-back measurement of the seat pan. The ergonomic target is 2–3 finger-widths of clearance between the front seat edge and the back of your knee (per Cornell Ergonomics Lab guidelines). At 6'0"–6'2", this requires roughly 17"–18.5" of seat depth. At 6'3"–6'5", 18.5"–20".</p>
        <p>Fixed-seat-depth chairs — even reinforced big-and-tall versions — are typically built at 17"–18" fixed depth, which is insufficient above 6'2". Adjustable seat depth is the feature that separates genuinely tall-user-appropriate chairs from wider-frame budget options. The Steelcase Leap Plus (17"–19.75" adjustable) and Gesture (16.75"–18.75" adjustable) both provide this. Most budget big-and-tall chairs do not.</p>

        <h3>3. Weight Capacity</h3>
        <p>Standard ergonomic chairs carry 250–300 lb. True big-and-tall chairs carry 400–500 lb. This matters independently of height — a taller frame carries more body weight on average, and a chair's structural limits are a separate safety question from its dimensional fit. Verify the weight capacity of any chair you're considering if you are near or above 250 lb.</p>
      </section>

