      <section>
        <h2>1. Steelcase Leap Plus — Best for Tall Users 6'3"+ Who Need High Weight Capacity</h2>
        <p><strong>Best for:</strong> Tall users 6'3"–6'7" who need both height range and structural capacity.<br/>
        <strong>Price:</strong> ~$1,400–$1,600 new; ~$600–$900 certified refurbished<br/>
        <strong>Key specs:</strong> 18"–22.5" seat height, 17"–19.75" adjustable seat depth, ~21" seat width, 25.5" back height, 500 lb weight capacity</p>

        <p>The Steelcase Leap Plus is the engineering-correct answer for tall users who are also concerned about weight capacity. It is the only mainstream ergonomic chair that simultaneously provides a 22.5" seat height ceiling, adjustable seat depth reaching 19.75", and a 500 lb structural rating. No other chair on this list achieves all three.</p>

        <p>The 22.5" seat height ceiling is the key differentiator. For a user at 6'5" with average proportions, the required seat height is approximately 21"–22". The Leap Plus is the only widely available chair that reaches that height — most competitors, including the Gesture and Aeron, max out at 20.5"–21". The adjustable seat depth (17"–19.75") handles users from 6'3" through 6'6"+ in most proportional cases.</p>

        <p>The weight capacity of 500 lb is structurally over-engineered for most users — it is better understood as a frame robustness indicator than a practical limit for the average buyer. It means the chair's welds, gas cylinder, and base are built to a significantly higher standard than chairs rated at 250–300 lb.</p>

