        <h2>3. Steelcase Gesture — Best All-Around for Tall Users 6'0"–6'3"</h2>
        <p><strong>Best for:</strong> Tall users 6'0"–6'3" who use multiple devices and change postures throughout the day.<br/>
        <strong>Price:</strong> ~$1,649 new; ~$400–$700 certified refurbished<br/>
        <strong>Key specs:</strong> 16"–21" seat height, 16.75"–18.75" adjustable seat depth, ~18.5" seat width, 400 lb weight capacity, 360-degree armrests</p>

        <p>The Gesture is the chair I own and use daily at 6'4". Its 21" seat height ceiling is borderline at my height — I use it at maximum — but it covers most users through about 6'3" with room for adjustment. The adjustable seat depth (16.75"–18.75") handles the 6'0"–6'3" range well in most proportional cases.</p>

        <p>The Gesture's distinguishing feature for tall users is the 360-degree rotating armrest system. Standard armrests pivot in one or two axes; the Gesture's armrests follow the arm through any position — rotated inward for typing, out and forward for tablet use, angled for reclined postures. For tall users whose upper-arm geometry doesn't match the assumed position of standard armrests, this matters more than marketing language typically conveys. I've found it eliminates the shoulder-elevation compensation that I needed in every previous chair.</p>

        <p>Full analysis: <a href="/review/gesture/" class="link-internal">Steelcase Gesture review — tested daily at 6'4" →</a></p>

