      <section>
        <h2>What "Big and Tall" Actually Means — and What It Doesn't</h2>
        <p>The "big and tall" label is a retail category, not an engineering specification. It typically means some combination of: increased weight capacity (usually 400–500 lb), wider seat pan, and taller back height. It does not guarantee an increased seat height range — which is the most important spec for tall users.</p>

        <p>A concrete example: a chair with a 24" back height, 400 lb capacity, and 20" seat height maximum is marketed as "big and tall." For a user who is 250 lb and 5'10", it may be appropriate. For a user who is 220 lb and 6'4", it fits neither need — the seat is too low, and the weight rating doesn't help with height. Always evaluate seat height maximum independently from weight capacity when filtering for "big and tall" chairs.</p>

        <p>The three specs to verify in order, regardless of marketing language:</p>
        <ol>
          <li><strong>Seat height maximum</strong> — must reach your required seated position (see <a href="/correct-chair-dimensions/" class="link-internal">correct chair dimensions by height →</a>)</li>
          <li><strong>Seat depth</strong> — fixed or adjustable, and whether the range covers your thigh length</li>
          <li><strong>Weight capacity</strong> — with margin above your actual weight</li>
        </ol>
      </section>

      <section>
        <h2>Height-Based Buying Guide</h2>

        <h3>6'0"–6'2": Multiple options cover the dimensional requirements</h3>
        <p>The Gesture (21" seat height, adjustable depth), Aeron Size C (20.5", fixed depth), La-Z-Boy Trafford (21", fixed depth), and OFM Essentials 200 (21.5", fixed depth) all provide adequate seat height for users in this range. Prioritize: adjustable seat depth above 17.5" if you have longer proportions; weight capacity if structural robustness matters; mesh construction if you run warm. Budget: Trafford or OFM Essentials. Mid-range: refurbished Leap V2 (~$400–$500). Premium: Gesture or Aeron Size C.</p>

        <h3>6'3"–6'4": Leap Plus or refurbished Gesture/Leap V2</h3>
        <p>The 20.5"–21" seat height ceiling of most chairs becomes constraining at 6'3"+. The Steelcase Leap Plus (22.5") is the primary new-chair recommendation. The Gesture at 21" is borderline — it works for many users at 6'3"–6'4" depending on proportions, but there is no adjustment headroom left. Refurbished Leap V2 units (~$400–$600) provide an adjustable seat depth that most budget big-and-tall chairs cannot match.</p>

        <h3>6'5"–6'7": Leap Plus is the primary option</h3>
        <p>The Leap Plus is the only mainstream chair engineered to fit users in the 6'5"–6'7" range on all three key dimensions simultaneously. At 6'5", the required seat height is approximately 21"–22" — the Leap Plus's 22.5" ceiling provides a workable fit. No budget big-and-tall chair provides adequate seat height in this range. See the <a href="/review/leap-plus/" class="link-internal">full Leap Plus review →</a></p>
      </section>

      <section>
        <h2>Frequently Asked Questions</h2>

        <h3>What is the best big and tall office chair for someone over 6 feet?</h3>
        <p>For 6'0"–6'3": the Steelcase Gesture or Herman Miller Aeron Size C covers most cases at the premium tier. For budget: La-Z-Boy Trafford or refurbished Leap V2. For 6'3"+: the <a href="/review/leap-plus/" class="link-internal">Steelcase Leap Plus</a> is the engineering-correct answer — it is the only mainstream chair with a 22.5" seat height ceiling and 500 lb capacity.</p>

        <h3>What weight capacity do big and tall office chairs have?</h3>
        <p>Budget big-and-tall chairs: 400 lb (Trafford, OFM). Premium: 350 lb (Aeron C), 400 lb (Gesture), 500 lb (Leap Plus). Standard ergonomic chairs are typically 250–300 lb. Verify the spec for any chair you're considering — "big and tall" does not guarantee a specific capacity.</p>

        <h3>Are big and tall chairs actually taller, or just rated for more weight?</h3>
        <p>Often just rated for more weight. Always check the seat height maximum independently — many chairs add 100–150 lb to the weight rating without changing the seat height range. The three specs to verify: seat height maximum, seat depth (and whether it is adjustable), and weight capacity.</p>
